me":"Thomsen, Jacob Bay","rank":44,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Nicolaj","key":"valstednicolajpau","last":"Valsted","name":"Valsted, Nicolaj","rank":45,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Anna","key":"vestergaardannapau","last":"Vestergaard","name":"Vestergaard, Anna","rank":46,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christoffer","key":"karoffchristofferpau","last":"Karoff","locid":"b71daf07-cedf-42bb-bd7c-b0af441e57cd","name":"Karoff, Christoffer","orcid":"0000-0003-2009-7965","rank":47,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3389/frsen.2024.1474560","issn":["26736187"],"journal":"Frontiers in Remote Sensing","paperid":"1474560","publisher":"Frontiers Media SA","status":"p","title":"Frontiers in Remote Sensing","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85208798743"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-ca4b833d-fa44-4572-9b87-8dda8c791fee","sdu-c53e86ae-0f83-4d4c-b84c-dc476842298f"],"src":["AU Aarhus University","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"DISCO-2 – an ambitious earth observing student CubeSat for arctic climate research","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-767862","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Time-restricted eating (TRE) has been suggested to be a simple, feasible, and effective dietary strategy for individuals with overweight or obesity. We aimed to investigate the effects of 3 months of 10-h per-day TRE and 3 months of follow-up on bodyweight and cardiometabolic risk factors in individuals at high risk of type 2 diabetes. METHODS: This was a single-centre, parallel, superiority, open-label randomised controlled clinical trial conducted at Steno Diabetes Center Copenhagen (Denmark). The inclusion criteria were age 30-70 years with either overweight (ie, BMI ≥25 kg/m2) and concomitant prediabetes (ie, glycated haemoglobin [HbA1c] 39-47 mmol/mol) or obesity (ie, BMI ≥30 kg/m2) with or without prediabetes and a habitual self-reported eating window (eating and drinking [except for water]) of 12 h per day or more every day and of 14 h per day or more at least 1 day per week. Individuals were randomly assigned 1:1 to 3 months of habitual living (hereafter referred to as the control group) or TRE, which was a self-selected 10-h per-day eating window placed between 0600 h and 2000 h. Randomisation was done in blocks varying in size and was open for participants and research staff, but outcome assessors were masked during statistical analyses. The randomisation list was generated by an external statistician. The primary outcome was change in bodyweight, assessed after 3 months (12 weeks) of the intervention and after 3 months (13 weeks) of follow-up. Adverse events were reported and registered at study visits or if participants contacted study staff to report events between visits. This trial is registered on ClinicalTrials.gov (NCT03854656). FINDINGS: Between March 12, 2019, and March 2, 2022, 100 participants (66 [66%] were female and 34 [34%] were male; median age 59 years [IQR 52-65]) were enrolled and randomly assigned (50 to each group). Of those 100, 46 (92%) in the TRE group and 46 (92%) in the control group completed the intervention period. After 3 months of the intervention, there was no difference in bodyweight between the TRE group and the control group (-0·8 kg, 95% CI -1·7 to 0·2; p=0·099). Being in the TRE group was not associated with a lower bodyweight compared with the control group after subsequent 3-month follow-up (-0·2 kg, -1·6 to 1·2). In the per-protocol analysis, participants who completed the intervention in the TRE group lost 1·0 kg (-1·9 to -0·0; p=0·040) bodyweight compared with the control group after 3 months of intervention, which was not maintained after the 3-month follow-up period (-0·4 kg, -1·8 to 1·0). During the trial and follow-up period, one participant in the TRE group reported a severe adverse event: development of a subcutaneous nodule and pain when the arm was in use. This side-effect was evaluated to be related to the trial procedures. INTERPRETATION: 3 months of 10-h per-day TRE did not lead to clinically relevant effects on bodyweight in middle-aged to older individuals at high risk of type 2 diabetes. FUNDING: Novo Nordisk Foundation, Aalborg University, Helsefonden, and Innovation Fund Denmark.","author_count":19,"contributors":["Quist, Jonas Salling","Pedersen, Hanne Enghoff","Jensen, Marie Møller","Clemmensen, Kim Katrine Bjerring","Bjerre, Natasja","Ekblond, Trine Spragge","Uldal, Sarah","Størling, Joachim","Wewer Albrechtsen, Nicolai J","Holst, Jens Juul","Torekov, Signe Sørensen","Nyeland, Martin Erik","Vistisen, Dorte","Jørgensen, Marit Eika","Panda, Satchidananda","Brock, Christina","Finlayson, Graham","Blond, Martin Bæk","Færch, Kristine"],"cuid":["S03-100.00-741523"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":3,"continent":["America, North","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Quist, Jonas Salling"],"country":["Greenland","United Kingdom","United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Salk Institute for Biological Studies","Salk Institute for Biological Studies, Torrey Pines, California, USA.","Steno Diabetes Center Greenland","University of Leeds"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Body Weight","Denmark epidemiology","Diabetes Mellitus Type 2 epidemiology","Female","Follow Up Studies","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Obesity epidemiology","Overweight"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-OT","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-STENO"],"OT":["Steno Diabetes Centre, Gentofte, Denmark."],"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-PR-OTH"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-HO-STENO","DI-PR-NOVO","DI-PR-OTH","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"effects of 000000000003 months of 000000000010 h per day time restricted eating and 000000000003 months of follow up on bodyweight and cardiometabolic health in danish individuals at high risk of type 000000000002 diabetes the reset single centre parallel superiority open label randomised controlled trial","src":["aau","ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S2666-7568(24)00028-X","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["This study was supported by an unrestricted grant from the Novo Nordisk Foundation (NNF17OC0027822), a PhD scholarship from Aalborg University, an industrial PhD scholarship from the Innovation Fund Denmark, and PhD funding from Helsefonden. We thank all study participants for their willingness to participate and Sara Sidenius, Camilla S\\u00F8s Nielsen, Nina Wittorff Jensen, and Hanne Vishof for excellent assistance and coordination. We thank Ann-Kristin Hansen, Helle N\\u00F8rg\\u00E5rd, Christina van Elst, Kaspar Persson, Malene Nygaard, Majken Lillholm Pico, Mette N\\u00F8rgaard J\\u00F8rgensen, and Nanna Fr\\u00F8lund for their assistance and contributions to the study. We thank Mathias Ried-Larsen for support in relation to analysis of accelerometer data."],"id":"S03-100.00-741523","identifier":[{"id":"38588687","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85189701290","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-bb6da8a7-4b0b-4f62-8e3b-6858a149f239","ku-70db8c8c-7373-46a6-823f-78862fdc224b","sdu-958bbd3f-48f4-49a6-977e-30d95d6889b4"],"keyword":["Adult","Aged","Body Weight","Denmark/epidemiology","Diabetes Mellitus, Type 2/epidemiology","Female","Follow-Up Studies","Humans","Male","Middle Aged","Obesity/epidemiology","Overweight"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-6-08ABAF498D489300E5F4D501F43B8675"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/708295754/Quist_et_al._2024_._Effects_of_3_months_of_10-h_per-day_time-restricted_eating_and_3_months_of_follow-up_on_bodyweight_and_cardiometabolic_health_in_Danish_individuals_at_high_risk_of_type_2_diabetes_the_RESET_single-centr.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/S2666-7568(24)00028-X","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/390821071/Fulltext.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Biomedical Sciences, Endocrinology and Metabolism"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"dk","key":"miscellaneous","l2":"DI-OT","l2n":"Miscellaneous","l3":"DI-OT","l3nl":"Other Research","l3nn":"Miscellaneous","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"na