{"abstract":"The tissue-specific release and uptake of metabolites in response to exercise is incompletely understood. Here, we detail a protocol to assess arteriovenous differences across the liver and hindlimb muscles in response to treadmill exercise in mice. We describe steps for the treadmill running of mice and the region-specific sampling of blood from the liver and hindlimb. This procedure is particularly relevant for the study of tissue-specific metabolism in response to exercise. For complete details on the use and execution of this protocol, please refer to Sato et al. (2022). 1.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Ashcroft, Stephen P","Jepsen, Sara L","Ehrlich, Amy M.","Treebak, Jonas T.","Holst, Jens Juul","Zierath, Juleen R"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-443811"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institutet"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"protocol to assess arteriovenous differences across the liver and hindlimb muscles in mice following treadmill exercise","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2022.101985","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-443811","identifier":[{"id":"36602899","type":"pmid"},{"id":"001058607000001","type":"wos"},{"id":"85146025135","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9826965","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-545ba77c-39c1-4e57-a3ba-f5568693be48"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-315BFF62CC587B14BF55BB889FA65835"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/333435474/1_s2.0_S2666166722008656_main.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xpro.2022.101985","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Zierath Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"karolinska institutet","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Karolinska Institutet","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Stephen P","key":"ashcroftpau","last":"Ashcroft","locid":"48c82ae8-3902-4c6c-83ab-fe24bd456ee0","name":"Ashcroft, Stephen P","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sara L","key":"jepsenpau","last":"Jepsen","locid":"01376a1a-125b-4cc7-8ad3-3497de4df56d","name":"Jepsen, Sara L","orcid":"0000-0003-1911-1563","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Amy M","key":"ehrlichpau","last":"Ehrlich","locid":"8bfbed8c-02f1-4c1d-b027-68f83ab9d319","name":"Ehrlich, Amy M.","name_org":"Ehrlich, Amy M","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas T","key":"treebakpau","last":"Treebak","locid":"a648ef82-ccb3-4add-83c1-384e0d90952b","name":"Treebak, Jonas T.","name_org":"Treebak, Jonas T","orcid":"0000-0003-1488-7012","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens J","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","name_org":"Holst, Jens J","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Juleen R","key":"zierathpau","last":"Zierath","locid":"f958ae7e-b12f-45b5-a44f-a11975cf1bbf","name":"Zierath, Juleen R","orcid":"0000-0001-6891-7497","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36602899"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.xpro.2022.101985","issn":["26661667"],"issue":"1","journal":"STAR Protocols","paperid":"101985","status":"p","title":"STAR Protocols","volume":"4"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85146025135"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-545ba77c-39c1-4e57-a3ba-f5568693be48"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Protocol to assess arteriovenous differences across the liver and hindlimb muscles in mice following treadmill exercise","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-443811","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Dysnatremia after congenital heart disease (CHD) surgery is common. European guidelines on intraoperative fluid therapy in children recommend isotonic solutions to avoid hyponatremia, but prolonged cardiopulmonary bypass and administration of high sodium-containing solutions (i.e., blood products and sodium bicarbonate) are associated with postoperative hypernatremia. The aim of the study was to describe fluid composition prior to and during the development of postoperative dysnatremia. A retrospective observational, single-center study including infants undergoing CHD surgery. Demographics and clinical characteristics were registered. Highest and lowest plasma sodium values were recorded and associations with perioperative fluid administration, blood products, crystalloids, and colloids were explored in relation to three perioperative periods. Postoperative dysnatremia occurred in nearly 50% of infants within 48 h after surgery. Hypernatremia was mainly associated with administration of blood products (median [IQR]: 50.5 [28.4–95.5] vs. 34.5 [18.5–61.1] mL/kg; p = 0.001), and lower free water load (1.6 [1.1–2.2] mL/kg/h; p = 0.01). Hyponatremia was associated with a higher free water load (2.3 [1.7–3.3] vs. 1.8 [1.4–2.5] mL/kg/h; p = 0.001) and positive fluid balance. On postoperative day 1, hyponatremia was associated with higher volumes of free water (2.0 [1.5–2.8] vs. 1.3 [1.1–1.8] mL/kg/h; p < 0.001) and human albumin, despite a larger diuresis and more negative daily fluid balance. Postoperative hyponatremia occurred in 30% of infants despite restrictive volumes of hypotonic maintenance fluid, whereas hypernatremia was mainly associated with blood product transfusion. Individualized fluid therapy, with continuous reassessment to reduce the occurrence of postoperative dysnatremia is mandatory in pediatric cardiac surgery. Prospective studies to evaluate fluid therapy in pediatric cardiac surgery patients are warranted.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Kronborg, Jonas Rønne","Lindhardt, Rasmus Bo","Vejlstrup, Niels","Holst, Line Marie","Juul, Klaus","Smerup, Morten Holdgaard","Gjedsted, Jakob","Ravn, Hanne Berg"],"cuid":["S02-088.24-686272"],"filters":{"added":"2023-08","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Kronborg, Jonas Rønne"],"keyword":["Child","Congenital heart disease","Fluid Therapy","Heart Defects Congenital surgery","Heart surgery","Humans","Hypernatremia","Hyponatremia","Hyponatremia epidemiology","Infant","Intensive care","Prospective Studies","Retrospective Studies","Sodium","Water","dysnatremia"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-CAP"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-CAP","DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"postoperative free water administration is associated with dysnatremia after congenital heart disease surgery in infants","src":["ku","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aas.14223","version":"Published Version"},"funding_acknowledgement":["Support was provided from Departmental sources and by a grant from The , Rigshospitalet, Copenhagen University Hospital, Denmark. Heart Centre"],"id":"S02-088.24-686272","identifier":[{"id":"85150645856","type":"scopus"},{"id":"36866603","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-6ebe4cea-1e36-4596-aa0c-294bde5978a3","sdu-7c708661-4c8b-4a68-bdc0-63d87492ee44"],"keyword":["Child","Heart Defects, Congenital/surgery","Humans","Hypernatremia/etiology","Hyponatremia/epidemiology","Infant","Prospective Studies","Retrospective Studies","Sodium","Water","congenital heart disease","dysnatremia","fluid therapy","heart surgery","hypernatremia","hyponatremia","intensive care"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-AC7C4FB7EDDE7BD39CA57B9816EBB086"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/363058426/Acta_Anaesthesiol_Scand_2023_Kronborg.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/aas.14223","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/226575901/Acta_Anaesthesiol_Scand_2023_Kronborg_Postoperative_free_water_administration_is_associated_with_dysnatremia_after.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"capital region of denmark","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-CAP","l3nl":"Capital Region of Denmark","l3nn":"Capital Region of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Capital Region of Denmark","namevar":["Copenhagen University Hospital - Rigshospitalet"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-CAP","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Cardiothoracic Surgery","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Clinical Research, KI, OUH, Research unit of Anaesthesiology and Intensive Care (Odense)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas Rønne","key":"kronborgpau","last":"Kronborg","name":"Kronborg, Jonas Rønne","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Bo","key":"lindhardtpau","last":"Lindhardt","name":"Lindhardt, Rasmus Bo","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Niels","key":"vejlstruppau","last":"Vejlstrup","name":"Vejlstrup, Niels","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Line Marie","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","name":"Holst, Line Marie","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Klaus","key":"juulpau","last":"Juul","name":"Juul, Klaus","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","first":"Morten Holdgaard","key":"smeruppau","last":"Smerup","locid":"633e94fc-6b5d-4f20-a683-bdd98c96ccdc","name":"Smerup, Morten","name_org":"Smerup, Morten Holdgaard","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jakob","key":"gjedstedpau","last":"Gjedsted","name":"Gjedsted, Jakob","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Hanne Berg","key":"ravnpau","last":"Ravn","locid":"25de7650-3dc1-4594-b758-9c36f6de6306","name":"Ravn, Hanne Berg","orcid":"0000-0003-4702-5195","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36866603"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/aas.14223","issn":["00015172"],"issue":"6","journal":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","pages":"730-737","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Acta Anaesthesiologica Scandinavica","volume":"67"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85150645856"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-6ebe4cea-1e36-4596-aa0c-294bde5978a3","sdu-7c708661-4c8b-4a68-bdc0-63d87492ee44"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen","SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Postoperative free water administration is associated with dysnatremia after congenital heart disease surgery in infants","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-088.24-686272","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"INTRODUCTION: The regenerative and immunomodulatory properties of multipotent mesenchymal stromal cells (MSCs) make them an intriguing asset for therapeutic applications. An off-the-shelf approach, using pre-expanded cryopreserved allogenic MSCs, bypasses many practical difficulties of cellular therapy. Reconstitution of a MSC product away from cytotoxic cryoprotectants towards a preferred administration solution might be favorable for several indications. Variations in MSC handling accompanied by a non-standardized use of reconstitution solutions complicate a general clinical standardization of MSC cellular therapies. In this study, we aimed to identify a simple and clinically compatible approach for thawing, reconstitution, and post-thaw storage of cryopreserved MSCs. METHODS: Human adipose tissue-derived MSCs were expanded in human platelet lysate (hPL) supplemented culture medium and cryopreserved using a dimethyl sulfoxide (DMSO)-based cryoprotectant. Isotonic solutions (saline, Ringer's acetate and phosphate buffered saline (PBS)) with or without 2% human serum albumin (HSA) were used as thawing, reconstitution, and storage solutions. MSCs were reconstituted to 5 × 10 6 MSCs/mL for evaluating MSC stability. Total MSC numbers and viability were determined using 7-aminoactinomycin D (7-AAD) and flow cytometry. RESULTS: For thawing cryopreserved MSCs the presence of protein was proven to be essential. Up to 50% of MSCs were lost when protein-free thawing solutions were used. Reconstitution and post-thaw storage of MSCs in culture medium and widely used PBS demonstrated poor MSC stability (>40% cell loss) and viability (<80%) after 1 h of storage at room temperature. Reconstitution in simple isotonic saline appeared to be a good alternative for post-thaw storage, ensuring >90% viability with no observed cell loss for at least 4 h. Reconstitution of MSCs to low concentrations was identified as critical. Diluting MSCs to <10 5/mL in protein-free vehicles resulted in instant cell loss (>40% cell loss) and lower viability (<80%). Addition of clinical grade HSA could prevent cell loss during thawing and dilution. CONCLUSION: This study identified a clinically compatible method for MSC thawing and reconstitution that ensures high MSC yield, viability, and stability. The strength of the method lies within the simplicity of implementation which offers an accessible way to streamline MSC therapies across different laboratories and clinical trials, improving standardization in this field.","author_count":8,"contributors":["Aabling, Rasmus Roost","Alstrup, Toke","Kjær, Emma Mader","Poulsen, Kristine Juul","Pedersen, Jonas Oute","Revenfeld, Anne Louise Schacht","Møller, Bjarne Kuno","Eijken, Marco"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-667851"],"filters":{"added":"2023-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Eijken, Marco"],"keyword":["Cell viability","Cellular therapy","Cryopreservation","Mesenchymal stromal cells","Reconstitution","Thawing"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"reconstitution and post thaw storage of cryopreserved human mesenchymal stromal cells pitfalls and optimizations for clinically compatible formulants","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.reth.2023.03.006","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-667851","identifier":[{"id":"PMC10154666","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"37153832","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85152647272","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-13b05787-2d07-4c11-8656-88e2d81cc0ed"],"keyword":["Cell viability","Cellular therapy","Cryopreservation","Mesenchymal stromal cells","Reconstitution","Thawing"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B1375BBDB0A2E9D91CC65AD880E207C4"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.reth.2023.03.006","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - Department of Clinical Immunology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rasmus Roost","key":"aablingpau","last":"Aabling","locid":"aec3b122-184f-47b6-9704-684077235766","name":"Aabling, Rasmus Roost","orcid":"0000-0003-2352-0597","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Toke","key":"alstruppau","last":"Alstrup","locid":"aa5ce4ef-b371-4a29-87c3-9fc4cfadfc5b","name":"Alstrup, Toke","orcid":"0000-0003-0540-2714","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Emma Mader","key":"kjærpau","last":"Kjær","name":"Kjær, Emma Mader","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Kristine Juul","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","name":"Poulsen, Kristine Juul","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas Oute","key":"pedersenpau","last":"Pedersen","name":"Pedersen, Jonas Oute","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anne Louise","key":"revenfeldpau","last":"Revenfeld","locid":"8bb500e3-790a-4014-9bc9-8fe575fa44c1","name":"Revenfeld, Anne Louise Schacht","name_org":"Revenfeld, Anne Louise","orcid":"0000-0003-1840-0259","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Bjarne Kuno","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","locid":"1efb651c-6d14-4c30-82e6-998dbb496ae0","name":"Møller, Bjarne Kuno","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Marco","key":"eijkenpau","last":"Eijken","locid":"fe12c770-d594-4332-96ae-2f1d8d9c0c01","name":"Eijken, Marco","orcid":"0000-0002-9043-3971","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37153832"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.reth.2023.03.006","issn":["23523204"],"journal":"Regenerative Therapy","pages":"67-75","publisher":"Japanese Society of Regenerative Medicine","status":"p","title":"Regenerative Therapy","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85152647272"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-13b05787-2d07-4c11-8656-88e2d81cc0ed"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Reconstitution and post-thaw storage of cryopreserved human mesenchymal stromal cells : Pitfalls and optimizations for clinically compatible formulants","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-667851","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"A significant proportion of events in paediatric acute myeloid leukaemia (AML) are caused by resistant disease (RD). We investigated clinical and biological characteristics in 66 patients with RD from 1013 children with AML registered and treated according to the NOPHO-AML 93, NOPHO-AML 2004, DB AML-01 and NOPHO-DBH AML 2012 protocols. Risk factors for RD were age10 years or older and a white-blood-cell count (WBC) of 100 × 10 9 /L or more at diagnosis. The five-year overall survival (OS) was 38% (95% confidence interval [CI]: 28%-52%). Of the 63 children that received salvage therapy with chemotherapy, 59% (N = 37) achieved complete remission (CR) with OS 57% (95% CI: 42%-75%) compared to 12% (95% CI: 4%-35%) for children that did not achieve CR. Giving more than two salvage chemotherapy courses did not increase CR rates. OS for all 43 patients receiving allogeneic haematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) was 49% (95% CI: 36%-66%). Those achieving CR and proceeding to HSCT had an OS of 56% (95% CI: 41%-77%, N = 30). This study showed that almost 40% of children with primary resistant AML can be cured with salvage therapy followed by HSCT. Children that did not achieve CR after two salvage courses with chemotherapy did not benefit from additional chemotherapy.","author_count":16,"contributors":["Karlsson, Lene","Cheuk, Daniel","De Moerloose, Barbara","Hasle, Henrik","Jahnukainen, Kirsi","Juul-Dam, Kristian Løvvik","Kaspers, Gertjan","Kovalova, Zanna","Lausen, Birgitte","Nyström, Ulrika Norén","Palle, Josefine","Pronk, Cornelis Jan","Saks, Kadri","Tierens, Anne","Zeller, Bernward","Abrahamsson, Jonas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-222799"],"filters":{"added":"2023-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["America, North","Asia, East","Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Karlsson, Lene"],"country":["Belgium","Canada","Finland","Germany","Hong Kong","Latvia","Netherlands","Norway","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Children's Clinical University Hospital","Ghent University","Hong Kong Children 's Hospital","Lund University","Oslo University Hospital","Princess Máxima Center for Pediatric Oncology","Samuel Lunenfeld Research Institute, Mount Sinai Hospital, Toronto","Technical University of Munich","Umeå University","University of Gothenburg","University of Helsinki","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Acute myeloid leukaemia","Paediatric","Survival"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"characteristics and outcome of primary resistant disease in paediatric acute myeloid leukaemia","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bjh.18685","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-222799","identifier":[{"id":"36762836","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85147567145","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-a4122e1b-d8b1-4b1c-ba0a-69dd9a3f17b2"],"keyword":["acute myeloid leukaemia","paediatric","resistant disease","survival"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-D3BAC5213A481F605BB5E7549BC224B0"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.au.dk/ws/files/401967246/Br_J_Haematol_-_2023_-_Karlsson_-_Characteristics_and_outcome_of_primary_resistant_disease_in_paediatric_acute_myeloid.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/bjh.18685","version":"Published Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"hk","key":"hong kong children s hospit