t":"Larsen","name":"Larsen, Jesper Juul","rank":10,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Barbara Juliane","key":"holzknechtpau","last":"Holzknecht","locid":"f0d0327c-3826-44aa-9fbc-87c759aec899","name":"Holzknecht, Barbara Juliane","rank":11,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas","key":"boelpau","last":"Boel","name":"Boel, Jonas","rank":12,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","first":"Pradeesh","key":"sivapalanpau","last":"Sivapalan","name":"Sivapalan, Pradeesh","rank":13,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 1","first":"Theis Skovsgaard","key":"itenovpau","last":"Itenov","locid":"0c35fe11-05aa-4ba4-97d6-b2d656781c60","name":"Itenov, Theis Skovsgaard","rank":14,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["37700929"],"pub":{"doi":"10.2147/clep.s413403","issn":["11791349"],"journal":"Clinical Epidemiology","pages":"939-955","status":"p","title":"Clinical Epidemiology","volume":"15"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85171350357"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","Capital Region of Denmark","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-c557941a-68a5-48c1-9a38-9e4f43e130b0"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Existing Data Sources in Clinical Epidemiology : Database of Community Acquired Infections Requiring Hospital Referral in Eastern Denmark (DCAIED) 2018–2021","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-732349","year":"2023","yearsub":"2023"}
{"abstract":"Diabetes incidence is rising globally at an accelerating rate causing issues at both the individual and societal levels. However, partly inspired by Ayurvedic medicine, a naturally occurring compound called pterostilbene has been demonstrated to protect against diabetes symptoms, though mainly in rats. The purpose of this study was to investigate the putative protective effect of pterostilbene on the two main aspects of diabetes, namely insulin resistance and decreased insulin secretion, in mice. To accomplish this, we employed diet-induced obese as well as streptozotocin-induced diabetic C57BL/6NTac mice for fasting glucose homeostasis assessment, tolerance tests and pancreas perfusions. In addition, we used the polygenic model of diabetes TALLYHO/JngJ to assess for prevention of β-cell burnout. We found that the diet-induced obese C57BL/6NTac mice were insulin resistant, but that pterostilbene had no impact on this or on overall glucose regulation. We further found that the reported protective effect of pterostilbene against streptozotocin-induced diabetes was absent in C57BL/6NTac mice, despite a promising pilot experiment. Lastly, we observed that pterostilbene does not prevent or delay onset of β-cell burnout in TALLYHO/JngJ mice. In conjunction with the literature, our findings suggest variations in the response to pterostilbene between species or between strains of species.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Damgaard, Mads V.","Jepsen, Sara L","Ashcroft, Stephen P","Holst, Jens Juul","Treebak, Jonas T."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-527148"],"filters":{"added":"2022-11","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Animals","Blood Glucose","Diabetes Mellitus Experimental drug therapy","Disease Models Animal","Glucose","Insulin metabolism","Insulin secretion","Mice","Mice Inbred C57BL","Mice Obese","Obesity drug therapy","Rats","Stilbenes","Streptozocin"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"pterostilbene fails to rescue insulin secretion and sensitivity in multiple murine models of diabetes","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14183741","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-527148","identifier":[{"id":"36145121","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85138750971","type":"scopus"},{"id":"PMC9505377","type":"pmcid"}],"ids":["ku-dfd29d0c-92f4-4d70-b3ab-2a92ea9ec2a6"],"keyword":["Animals","Blood Glucose","Diabetes Mellitus, Experimental/drug therapy","Disease Models, Animal","Glucose","Insulin Secretion","Insulin/metabolism","Mice","Mice, Inbred C57BL","Mice, Obese","Obesity/drug therapy","Rats","Stilbenes","Streptozocin"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-1BFDBA711E450627BFCFDEC58EDC74B9"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/333633753/Pterostilbene_Fails_to_Rescue_Insulin_Secretion.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/nu14183741","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Treebak Group"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","first":"Mads V","key":"damgaardpau","last":"Damgaard","locid":"39636497-0085-491e-8969-847d7a04bdf1","name":"Damgaard, Mads V.","name_org":"Damgaard, Mads V","orcid":"0000-0001-8488-2298","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Sara L","key":"jepsenpau","last":"Jepsen","locid":"01376a1a-125b-4cc7-8ad3-3497de4df56d","name":"Jepsen, Sara L","orcid":"0000-0003-1911-1563","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Stephen P","key":"ashcroftpau","last":"Ashcroft","locid":"48c82ae8-3902-4c6c-83ab-fe24bd456ee0","name":"Ashcroft, Stephen P","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jens J","key":"holstpau","last":"Holst","locid":"864b6401-9024-4773-9bdb-f4d4ca57dcad","name":"Holst, Jens Juul","name_org":"Holst, Jens J","orcid":"0000-0001-6853-3805","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Jonas T","key":"treebakpau","last":"Treebak","locid":"a648ef82-ccb3-4add-83c1-384e0d90952b","name":"Treebak, Jonas T.","name_org":"Treebak, Jonas T","orcid":"0000-0003-1488-7012","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["36145121"],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/nu14183741","issn":["20726643"],"issue":"18","journal":"Nutrients","paperid":"3741","status":"p","title":"Nutrients","volume":"14"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85138750971"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-dfd29d0c-92f4-4d70-b3ab-2a92ea9ec2a6"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Pterostilbene Fails to Rescue Insulin Secretion and Sensitivity in Multiple Murine Models of Diabetes","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-527148","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"Introduction. Adults with generalised joint hypermobility including knee joint hypermobility (GJHk) report more knee joint symptoms when compared to adults without GJHk. There is no consensus on best practice for symptom management. For instance, controversy exists regarding the appropriateness and safety of heavy resistance training as an intervention for this specific group. This case series aims to describe a supervised, progressive heavy resistance training program in adults with GJHk and knee pain, the tolerability of the intervention, and the outcomes of knee pain, knee-related quality of life, muscle strength, proprioception, and patellar tendon stiffness through a 12-week period. Materials and Methods. Adults with GJHk and knee pain were recruited to perform supervised, progressive heavy resistance training twice a week for 12 weeks. The main outcome was the tolerability of the intervention. Secondary outcomes were knee pain during a self-nominated activity (VASNA); Knee injury and Osteoarthritis Outcome Score (KOOS); Tampa Scale of Kinesiophobia (TSK); maximal quadriceps voluntary isometric contraction and rate of torque development; 5 repetition maximum strength in five different leg exercises; single leg hop for distance; knee proprioception and patellar tendon stiffness. Results. In total, 16 women (24.2 years, SD 2.5) completed at least 21/24 training sessions. No major adverse events were observed. On average, VASNA decreased by 32.5 mm (95% CI 21.4–43.6), in addition to improvements in KOOS and TSK scores. These improvements were supported by an increase in all measures of lower extremity muscle strength, knee proprioception, and patellar tendon stiffness. Conclusion. Supervised heavy resistance training seems to be well tolerated and potentially beneficial in young women with GJHk and knee pain.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Henriksen, Peter","Junge, Tina","Bojsen-Møller, Jens","Juul-Kristensen, Birgit","Thorlund, Jonas Bloch"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-048563"],"filters":{"added":"2023-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"corresponding_author":["Henriksen, Peter"],"keyword":["Hypermobilitet","Knæ","Physiotherapy","Senestivhed","Smerte","Styrketræning"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-UC"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"],"UC":["DI-UC-ALL","DI-UC-UCL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCL"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"supervised heavy resistance training is tolerated and potentially beneficial in women with knee pain and knee joint hypermobility a case series","src":["sdu","ucl"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU","DI-UC-UCL"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/8367134","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-048563","identifier":[{"id":"38655168","type":"pmid"},{"id":"PMC11022762","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"85145988713","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-109da3ce-79c8-4ecd-b699-f32d04c030d4","ucl-e4b4afc8-6c19-4816-acb1-a56eaf57df32"],"keyword":["Hypermobilitet","Knæ","Senestivhed","Smerte","Styrketræning","physiotherapy"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-36DD82B1719ADFBB313805FDCE6E849B"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://findresearcher.sdu.dk/ws/files/220390420/8367134.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1155/2022/8367134","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2022","yearSub":"2022"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ucl university college","l2":"DI-UC","l2n":"University Colleges","l3":"DI-UC-UCL","l3nl":"UCL University College","l3nn":"UCL University College","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"UCL University College","namevar":["UCL Erhvervsakademi og Professionshøjskole, Direktion, Sundhed, Fysioterapeutuddannelsen"],"nora_code":"DI-UC-UCL","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Sports Science and Clinical Biomechanics, Research Unit of Musculoskeletal Function and Physiotherapy"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Peter","key":"henriksenpau","last":"Henriksen","locid":"694aee1d-b427-4a91-9012-90f8f60500e1","name":"Henriksen, Peter","orcid":"0000-0002-7706-7137","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Tina","key":"jungepau","last":"Junge","locid":"248f2025-f601-4572-adb6-13dc9a92b598","name":"Junge, Tina","orcid":"0000-0002-3304-3010","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Jens","key":"bojsenmøllerpau","last":"Bojsen-Møller","locid":"0b8727d1-a7f3-4ab6-bcb2-e836c0b320f3","name":"Bojsen-Møller, Jens","orcid":"0000-0001-6737-446X","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Birgit","key":"juulkristensenpau","last":"Juul-Kristensen","locid":"887ddfe7-cfa6-4b72-a17d-ecf86987c5c0","name":"Juul-Kristensen, Birgit","orcid":"0000-0002-4973-8415","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Jonas Bloch","key":"thorlundpau","last":"Thorlund","locid":"5b59bfe4-8aff-41d8-8b2d-4b19ab3e56c4","name":"Thorlund, Jonas Bloch","orcid":"0000-0001-7789-8224","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38655168"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1155/2022/8367134","issn":["25738488"],"journal":"Translational Sports Medicine","paperid":"8367134","publisher":"Wiley","status":"p","title":"Translational Sports Medicine","volume":"2022"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85145988713"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","All University Colleges","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","DI-UC","DI-UC-ALL","UCL University College","Universities","University Colleges","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-109da3ce-79c8-4ecd-b699-f32d04c030d4","ucl-e4b4afc8-6c19-4816-acb1-a56eaf57df32"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark","UCL University College"],"title":"Supervised, Heavy Resistance Training Is Tolerated and Potentially Beneficial in Women with Knee Pain and Knee Joint Hypermobility: A Case Series","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S02-100.00-048563","year":"2022","yearsub":"2022"}
{"abstract":"OBJECTIVE: The glucose tolerance test (GTT) is a widely used assay in preclinical research to interrogate glucose metabolism, but there is no standardised way by which glucose is administered. The aim of this study was to directly compare the effect of the route of glucose administration on glucose and insulin kinetics during a GTT in mice. METHODS: A GTT was performed in lean male and female mice and obese male mice and glucose was administered by either the oral or intraperitoneal (I.P.) route. Frequent samples were taken during the GTT to provide a full time-course of the insulin and glucose excursions. In another cohort of lean male mice, plasma concentrations of insulin, c-peptide and incretin hormones were measured at early timepoints after glucose administration. A stable-isotope labelled GTT was then performed to delineate the contribution of exogenous and endogenous glucose to glycaemia during a GTT, comparing both methods of glucose administration. Finally, we present a method to easily measure insulin from small volumes of blood during a GTT by directly assaying whole-blood insulin by ELISA and show a good concordance between whole-blood and plasma insulin measurements. RESULTS: We report that I.P. glucose administration results in an elevated blood glucose excursion and a largely absent elevation in blood insulin and plasma incretin hormones when compared to oral administration. Utilising stable-isotope labelled glucose, we demonstrate that the difference in glucose excursion between the two routes of administration is mainly due to a lack of suppression of glucose production in I.P. injected mice. Additionally, rates of exogenous glucose appearance into circulation are different between lean and obese mice after I.P., but not after oral glucose administration. CONCLUSION: Reflecting on these data, we suggest that careful consideration be given to the route of glucose administration when planning a GTT procedure in mice and suggest that in most circumstances the oral route of glucose administration should be preferred over the I.P. route to avoid possible artifacts originating from a non-physiological route.","author_count":11,"contributors":["Small, Lewin","Ehrlich, Amy M.","Iversen, Jo","Ashcroft, Stephen P","Trošt, Kajetan","Moritz, Thomas","Hartmann, Bolette","Holst, Jens Juul","Treebak, Jonas T.","Zierath, Juleen R","Barrès, Romain"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-405938"],"filters":{"added":"2022-03","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["France","Sweden"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Karolinska Institutet","Swedish University of Agricultural Sciences","University of Côte d'Azur"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-PR","DI-U"],"level3":{"PR":["DI-PR-ALL","DI-PR-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-PR-NOVO","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"comparative analysis of oral and intraperitoneal glucose tolerance tests in mice","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molmet.2022.101440","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-405938","identifier":[{"id":"PMC8810558","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"35026435","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85123699594","type":"scopus"},{"id":"000788014200015","type":"wos"}],"ids":["ku-14f23c5b-87b8-44b6-a350-08731fa49c8d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-66FC900CB0AE87F878DBEE0037E920CC"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"loc","url":"https://curis.ku.dk/ws/files/291221338/1_s2.0_S2212877822000096.pdf","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.molmet.2022.101440","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Medic