{"abstract":"BACKGROUND: Thinner and shorter needles for subcutaneous administration are continuously developed. Previous studies have shown that a thinner needle causes fewer occurrences of painful needle insertions and that a shorter needle decreases the occurrence of painful intramuscular injections. However, little is known about local drug delivery in relation to needle length and thickness. This study aimed to compare deposition depth and backflow from three hypodermic needles of 3 mm 34G (0.19 mm), 5 mm 32G (0.23 mm), and 8 mm 30G (0.30 mm) in length and thickness. METHODS: Ex vivo experiments were carried out on pigs, in neck tissue comparable to human skin at typical injection sites. Six pigs were included and a total of 72 randomized injections were given, i.e. 24 subcutaneous injections given with each needle type. Accordingly, 400 µL was injected including 70% NovoRapid(®) (Novo Nordisk A/S, Bagsvµrd, Denmark) and 30% Xenetix(®) (Guerbet, Villepinte, France) contrast including 1 mg/mL Alcian blue. Surgical biopsies of injection sites were sampled and computer topographic (CT)-scanned in 3D to assess deposition and local distribution. Biopsies were prepared and stained to evaluate deposition in comparison to the CT-scanning findings. The backflow of each injection was collected with filter paper. The blue stains of filter paper were digitized and volume estimated by software calculation vs. control staining. RESULTS: CT-scanning (n = 57) and histology (n = 10) showed that, regardless of injection depth, the bulk of the injection was in the subcutaneous tissue and did not propagate from subcutis into dermis. With the 8 mm 30G needle all injections apart from one intramuscular injection were located in the subcutaneous layer. The volume depositions peaked in 4-5 mm depth for the 3 mm 34G needle, in 5-6 mm depth for the 5 mm 32G needle, and in 9-10 mm depth for the 8 mm 30G needle. In general, injection depositions evaluated by histology and CT-scans compared well for the individual biopsies. The amount of backflow (n = 69) from the 3 mm 34G needle was smaller (P ","author_count":8,"contributors":["Ann Praestmark Juul, Kezia","Bengtsson, Henrik","Eyving, Bente","Kildegaard, Jonas","Lav, Steffen","Poulsen, Mette Homann","Serup, Jørgen","Stallknecht, Bente"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-450352"],"filters":{"added":"2022-04","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"influence of hypodermic needle dimensions on subcutaneous injection delivery a pig study of injection deposition evaluated by ct scanning histology and backflow","src":["ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0846.2011.00592.x","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-450352","identifier":[{"id":"84867533133","type":"scopus"},{"id":"22233448","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["ku-5f17e297-e001-44af-b9aa-ca23c391f62d"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-345796F61AD6C9785ADE10BD4AEEA4B5"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1600-0846.2011.00592.x","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Pharmaceutical Sciences, Department of Drug Design and Pharmacology"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"","first":"Kezia","key":"annpraestmarkjuulpau","last":"Ann Praestmark Juul","name":"Ann Praestmark Juul, Kezia","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Henrik","key":"bengtssonpau","last":"Bengtsson","name":"Bengtsson, Henrik","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Bente","key":"eyvingpau","last":"Eyving","name":"Eyving, Bente","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jonas","key":"kildegaardpau","last":"Kildegaard","name":"Kildegaard, Jonas","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Steffen","key":"lavpau","last":"Lav","name":"Lav, Steffen","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Mette","key":"poulsenpau","last":"Poulsen","locid":"2e521080-f37a-4585-94ec-b91feb2baeec","name":"Poulsen, Mette Homann","name_org":"Poulsen, Mette","orcid":"0000-0003-2162-3007","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Jørgen","key":"seruppau","last":"Serup","locid":"a2144be9-2382-444c-a88c-cb211bb74c1a","name":"Serup, Jørgen","rank":7,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","first":"Bente Merete","key":"stallknechtpau","last":"Stallknecht","locid":"eddf86eb-44d6-44ca-985b-79a29dd3cec7","name":"Stallknecht, Bente","name_org":"Stallknecht, Bente Merete","orcid":"0000-0003-3117-0451","rank":8,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["22233448"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/j.1600-0846.2011.00592.x","issn":["0909752X","16000846"],"issn_org":["0909752X"],"issue":"4","journal":"Skin Research and Technology","pages":"447-455","status":"p","title":"Skin Research and Technology","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84867533133"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities","University of Copenhagen"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["ku-5f17e297-e001-44af-b9aa-ca23c391f62d"],"src":["KU University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Influence of hypodermic needle dimensions on subcutaneous injection delivery - a pig study of injection deposition evaluated by CT scanning, histology, and backflow","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-450352","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
{"abstract":"GLP-1 is an insulinotropic hormone that synergistically with glucose gives rise to an increased insulin response. Its secretion is increased following a meal and it is thus of interest to describe the secretion of this hormone following an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT). The aim of this study was to build a mechanism-based population model that describes the time course of total GLP-1 and provides indices for capability of secretion in each subject. The goal was thus to model the secretion of GLP-1, and not its effect on insulin production. Single 75 g doses of glucose were administered orally to a mixed group of subjects ranging from healthy volunteers to patients with type 2 diabetes (T2D). Glucose, insulin, and total GLP-1 concentrations were measured. Prior population data analysis on measurements of glucose and insulin were performed in order to estimate the glucose absorption rate. The individual estimates of absorption rate constants were used in the model for GLP-1 secretion. Estimation of parameters was performed using the FOCE method with interaction implemented in NONMEM VI. The final transit/indirect-response model obtained for GLP-1 production following an OGTT included two stimulation components (fast, slow) for the zero-order production rate. The fast stimulation was estimated to be faster than the glucose absorption rate, supporting the presence of a proximal–distal loop for fast secretion from l-cells. The fast component (st 3 = 8.64·10−5 [mg−1]) was estimated to peak around 25 min after glucose ingestion, whereas the slower component (st 4 = 26.2·10−5 [mg−1]) was estimated to peak around 100 min. Elimination of total GLP-1 was characterised by a first-order loss. The individual values of the early phase GLP-1 secretion parameter (st 3 ) were correlated (r = 0.52) with the AUC(0–60 min.) for GLP-1. A mechanistic population model was successfully developed to describe total GLP-1 concentrations over time observed after an OGTT. The model provides indices related to different mechanisms of subject abilities to secrete GLP-1. The model provides a good basis to study influence of different demographic factors on these components, presented mainly by indices of the fast- and slow phases of GLP-1 response.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Møller, Jonas B.","Jusko, William J.","Gao, Wei","Hansen, Torben","Pedersen, Oluf","Holst, Jens J.","Overgaard, Rune V.","Madsen, Henrik","Ingwersen, Steen H."],"cuid":["S02-098.79-035120"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["America, North","OECD"],"country":["United States"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","SUNY - The State University of New York"],"level":"sci","mra":["med","sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-NP","DI-U"],"level3":{"NP":["DI-NP-ALL","DI-NP-NOVO"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"level3all":["DI-NP-NOVO","DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"mechanism based population modelling for assessment of l cell function based on total glp 000000000001 response following an oral glucose tolerance test","src":["dtu","ku"],"src-code":["DI-U-DTU","DI-U-KU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10928-011-9216-2","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-098.79-035120","identifier":[{"id":"PMC3407884","type":"pmcid"},{"id":"21922329","type":"pmid"},{"id":"84855680173","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["dtu-6e406731-8e11-4485-9e43-dd6340f3a37d","ku-bec819ae-7994-44f5-ab51-d59e26c559cd"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science","Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-5-E59536E3836E7AB1AFA32A3470C50175"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1007/s10928-011-9216-2","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"novo nordisk foundation","l2":"DI-NP","l2n":"Non-Profit Organisations","l3":"DI-NP-NOVO","l3nn":"Novo Nordisk Foundation","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Novo Nordisk Foundation","namevar":["Novo Nordisk Foundation"],"nora_code":"DI-NP-NOVO","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"us","key":"suny the state university of new york","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"SUNY - The State University of New York","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"ku university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-KU","l3nl":"University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"KU University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"KU University of Copenhagen","namevar":["Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Center Management","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, Hansen Group","Københavns Universitet, Faculty of Health and Medical Sciences, Novo Nordisk Foundation Center for Basic Metabolic Research, NNF Center for Basic Metabolic Research"],"nora_code":"DI-U-KU","used":1},{"affno":"4","key":"dtu technical university of denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-DTU","l3nl":"Technical University of Denmark","l3nn":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"DTU Technical University of Denmark","namevar":["Technical University of Denmark, Department of Informatics and Mathematical Modeling","Technical University of Denmark, Department of Informatics and Mathematical Modeling, Mathematical Statistics"],"nora_code":"DI-U-DTU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jonas B.","key":"møllerpau","last":"Møller","name":"Møller, Jonas B.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"William J.","key":"juskopau","last":"Jusko","name":"Jusko, William J.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","cor